1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,212
Welcome to Module Twelve.

2
00:00:02,212 --> 00:00:04,362
A food chain is a line.

3
00:00:04,362 --> 00:00:07,387
Grass, grasshopper, shrew, owl.

4
00:00:07,387 --> 00:00:09,987
But you know that real communities are webs.

5
00:00:09,987 --> 00:00:13,625
Several consumers feed on the same resource at once.

6
00:00:13,625 --> 00:00:15,612
Flows split and merge.

7
00:00:15,612 --> 00:00:18,500
One compartment's output is another's input.

8
00:00:18,500 --> 00:00:24,612
And the moment you try to draw a web, you are forced to say exactly what is connected to what, and by which flow.

9
00:00:24,612 --> 00:00:28,762
Yesterday you built a loop that makes one piece of work reliable.

10
00:00:28,762 --> 00:00:31,900
Today you draw the web around many pieces.

11
00:00:31,900 --> 00:00:33,062
What runs.

12
00:00:33,062 --> 00:00:34,875
What runs at the same time.

13
00:00:34,875 --> 00:00:36,237
What has to wait.

14
00:00:36,237 --> 00:00:38,025
Where results come together.

15
00:00:38,025 --> 00:00:39,612
And where they are checked.

16
00:00:39,612 --> 00:00:41,912
Builders call this graph engineering.

17
00:00:41,912 --> 00:00:43,675
There are only two parts.

18
00:00:43,675 --> 00:00:48,075
A node is one bounded job, with one input and one output.

19
00:00:48,075 --> 00:00:50,487
An edge is a real dependency.

20
00:00:50,487 --> 00:00:53,250
This step's output feeds that step's input.

21
00:00:53,250 --> 00:00:56,912
And here is the most useful question in today's module.

22
00:00:56,912 --> 00:01:03,275
For every arrow in your workflow, ask: does the next step actually read the previous step's output?

23
00:01:03,275 --> 00:01:05,787
Can you name the thing that crosses the arrow?

24
00:01:05,787 --> 00:01:08,662
If you cannot, there is no real edge.

25
00:01:08,662 --> 00:01:13,650
The second step was only waiting because of the order you happened to type the instructions.

26
00:01:13,650 --> 00:01:22,625
One writer redrew a forty-step workflow this way, cut about a dozen fake arrows, and it ran far faster, with no change to the model at all.

27
00:01:22,625 --> 00:01:25,062
The vocabulary of nodes is small.

28
00:01:25,062 --> 00:01:27,512
A splitter cuts the work into pieces.

29
00:01:27,512 --> 00:01:32,112
Workers each handle one piece, in their own separate context.

30
00:01:32,112 --> 00:01:39,487
A plain code node merges, sorts and removes duplicates, because those jobs have one right answer and need no model at all.

31
00:01:39,487 --> 00:01:42,137
And a gate decides what passes.

32
00:01:42,137 --> 00:01:45,012
The gate brings today's most important lesson.

33
00:01:45,012 --> 00:01:47,437
Do not let the worker grade its own work.

34
00:01:47,437 --> 00:01:49,887
Models tend to favour their own writing.

35
00:01:49,887 --> 00:01:55,937
So the checker must be separate, and it must start fresh, without seeing the worker's reasoning.

36
00:01:55,937 --> 00:01:58,862
Even then, anchor it to something real.

37
00:01:58,862 --> 00:02:00,912
A test that actually ran.

38
00:02:00,912 --> 00:02:02,562
A rule written in code.

39
00:02:02,562 --> 00:02:05,600
Finally, a good graph has two ways back.

40
00:02:05,600 --> 00:02:11,087
A short one, which returns a single failed piece to the worker that made it, with the evidence.

41
00:02:11,087 --> 00:02:16,225
And a long one, which turns a lesson into a rule for how the next run is split.

42
00:02:16,225 --> 00:02:18,600
Almost nobody builds that second one.

43
00:02:18,600 --> 00:02:20,625
Now, where does the metaphor fail?

44
00:02:20,625 --> 00:02:23,437
Energy flows one way down a food chain.

45
00:02:23,437 --> 00:02:30,637
In an agent graph, information flows backwards on purpose, and the learning edge rewrites the web itself.

46
00:02:30,637 --> 00:02:32,487
Nothing in nature does that.

47
00:02:32,487 --> 00:02:35,450
And a food web is discovered, not designed.

48
00:02:35,450 --> 00:02:39,200
Draw your graph too early, and you can lock in your first wrong guess.

49
00:02:39,200 --> 00:02:46,600
In today's lab you will build a quality-control graph for thirty invented field records, with ten faults you planted yourself.

50
00:02:46,600 --> 00:02:56,112
Workers check batches in parallel, plain code merges them, rules and a fresh verifier form the gate, and a report scores which part caught what.

51
00:02:56,112 --> 00:02:59,275
Then you add one learning edge, and run it again.

52
00:02:59,275 --> 00:03:02,025
Open your terminal, and let's draw the web.

